    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. ok so i read somewhere that blockages in the tube are a huge safety risk. what kind of stuff usually clogs them up and how does that affect the reading?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Pitot tubes can become blocked by insects, mud/dust, ice, or even debris from a runway wash‑down; the static ports can foul with spider webs, wax, or frost. If the **pitot opening** is blocked while the static port remains open, the pitot pressure freezes at the last value, so the indicator reads **zero or a very low speed** (a “pitot‑blocked” low‑air‑speed condition). If the **static ports** are blocked and the pitot stays open, the gauge sees only the increasing pitot pressure as the aircraft accelerates, causing the airspeed indicator to **over‑read dramatically**, possibly leading to an overspeed warning or false stall indication. Simultaneous blockage of both can lock the instrument at a fixed reading, which is why pilots perform pre‑flight checks and why heated pitot lines are used on many aircraft.",
      "source": "target",
